ANSIBLE FORKS 
====================================================================

--------------------------------------------------
WHAT ARE FORKS?
--------------------------------------------------

Forks determine how many managed hosts
Ansible can connect to and execute tasks on
at the same time (in parallel).

Default:

    5 forks

Meaning:

    By default, Ansible can execute a task
    simultaneously on 5 servers.


====================================================================
WHY DO WE NEED FORKS?
====================================================================

Suppose you have:

    100 Linux servers

Task:

    Install Nginx

Without parallel execution:

    Server1
      ↓
    Server2
      ↓
    Server3
      ↓
    ...
      ↓
    Server100

Very slow ❌


With Forks = 10

    Server1      Server6

    Server2      Server7

    Server3      Server8

    Server4      Server9

    Server5      Server10

All execute simultaneously ✅


====================================================================
DEFAULT VALUE
====================================================================

Default forks:

    5

Meaning:

If you have

20 servers

Ansible works like:

Batch 1

    5 Servers

↓

Batch 2

    5 Servers

↓

Batch 3

    5 Servers

↓

Batch 4

    5 Servers


====================================================================
HOW TO CHANGE FORKS?
====================================================================

Method 1

Command Line

ansible-playbook site.yml --forks 20


--------------------------------------------------
Method 2

ansible.cfg

forks = 20


Example

[defaults]

forks = 20


====================================================================
REAL-TIME EXAMPLE
====================================================================

Company has

200 EC2 servers

Need to install Docker.

Using

forks = 5

Execution takes

15 minutes

Using

forks = 50

Execution takes

2 minutes

Huge performance improvement.


====================================================================
HOW FORKS WORK
====================================================================

Suppose

12 Servers

Forks = 4

Execution

Round 1

Server1

Server2

Server3

Server4

↓

Round 2

Server5

Server6

Server7

Server8

↓

Round 3

Server9

Server10

Server11

Server12


====================================================================
ADVANTAGES
====================================================================

• Faster execution

• Parallel processing

• Better automation

• Saves deployment time


====================================================================
DISADVANTAGES
====================================================================

Too many forks

↓

High CPU usage

↓

High Memory usage

↓

Too many SSH connections

↓

Can overload Ansible Control Node


====================================================================
REAL DEVOPS USE CASE
====================================================================

Company has

500 Web Servers

Need to

Install Security Patch

Without forks

Several hours

With forks = 50

Completed within minutes



====================================================================
FORKS vs SERIAL (IMPORTANT)
====================================================================

Forks

    Controls how many hosts Ansible
    can connect to in parallel.

Example

100 Servers

forks = 20

↓

20 servers execute simultaneously.


-----------------------------------------------

Serial

Controls deployment batches.

Example

serial = 10

↓

Deploy to first 10 servers

↓

Then next 10

↓

Then next 10

Used for rolling deployments.


====================================================================
FORKS vs SERIAL
====================================================================

Forks

    Parallel execution

Serial

    Batch execution


====================================================================
BEST PRACTICES
====================================================================

Small Environment

    forks = 5

Medium Environment

    forks = 10-20

Large Production

    forks = 30-50

Choose a value based on:

    • CPU
    • RAM
    • Network
    • Number of servers

Avoid setting forks too high unless your
control node has enough resources.

